From 303038751932badb72e439e01aa630ae970f0493 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rou <shamay1030@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 18:34:15 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/27] Stop evaluating disabled trace arguments in the KV-link
 path

_emit_pic_trace returns immediately when SGLANG_PIC_TRACE_PATH is unset, but
Python evaluates its keyword arguments first. Eighteen call sites passed
_trace_values(<tensor>), whose body is

    values.detach().reshape(-1).cpu().tolist()

and .cpu() on a CUDA tensor is a device-to-host copy that synchronises the
stream. One site also built its argument with torch.cat, allocating a tensor per
repair unit. Profiler-free ABBA runs are this project's formal latency evidence
and they run with tracing disabled, so that synchronisation and allocation sat
inside the measured path unconditionally.

_trace_values now returns a _LazyTraceValues holding the source, and
_lazy_trace defers an arbitrary expensive field; _emit_pic_trace materializes
them recursively immediately after the enabled check, so a written record holds
exactly what it held before. A callable source also defers the work that
produces the values, which is what removes the torch.cat and the
_contiguous_ranges walk over every repair position.

Measured on RTX 5070 with Qwen2.5-3B, feal-linear-cryptanalysis:

- one CUDA _trace_values costs 44 us at n=128 and 161 us at n=3654, the floor
  being the sync itself;
- each link request carried 11 such fields over 12,906 elements, about 0.9 ms;
- with tracing disabled the call is now 0.68 us with zero materializations.

This is a measurement-hygiene and code-quality fix, not a speedup: 0.9 ms
against a 1.6 s request is roughly 0.06% and will not be visible end to end.
What it removes is GPU synchronisation that disabled instrumentation had no
business performing inside a measured path.

Behaviour verified unchanged on hardware with tracing enabled: 29 event types
before and after, zero field differences, and the deferred fields byte-identical.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
